Explaination:-

1. First command: installing dnsutils

You ran:

sudo apt install dnsutils

Ubuntu replied:

Note, selecting 'bind9-dnsutils' instead of 'dnsutils'

This simply means:

On your version of Ubuntu, the package called dnsutils is provided/handled through bind9-dnsutils.

Then:

bind9-dnsutils is already the newest version

means:

It is already installed on your computer.

So nothing needed to be installed.

This part:

Upgrading: 0, Installing: 0, Removing: 0

confirms that.

So your first part is completely fine. 

2. Now the important part: nslookup google.com

You ran:

nslookup google.com

And got:

Server:         127.0.0.53
Address:        127.0.0.53#53

This is exactly what we were discussing.

Let's translate it into simple English.

Server: 127.0.0.53

It means:

Your Ubuntu is asking the local DNS service at 127.0.0.53 to resolve google.com.

Remember:

127.0.0.53
      ↓
local DNS service

It is running on your own Ubuntu machine.

3. What does #53 mean?

You have:

127.0.0.53#53

The #53 means port 53.

DNS normally uses:

Port 53

So you can read:

127.0.0.53#53

as:

DNS service at 127.0.0.53, using port 53.

For now, just remember:

DNS → port 53

4. Now look at this

You got:

Non-authoritative answer:

Don't worry about this yet.

It sounds complicated, but the simple meaning is:

The DNS server that answered you is not the original/official DNS server responsible for google.com.

It got the answer from somewhere else, or from its cache/forwarding process.

For your beginner DNS learning, you can simply remember:

Non-authoritative answer
        ↓
"I can give you the answer,
but I'm not the official owner of this DNS information."

5. Now the most important part

You asked:

google.com

Your DNS system returned:

Name: google.com
Address: 192.178.134.100
Name: google.com
Address: 192.178.134.101
...

This means:

google.com → 192.178.134.100
google.com → 192.178.134.101
google.com → 192.178.134.102
...

So Google has multiple IP addresses being returned for the name google.com.

Why multiple?

Because large services such as Google have many servers and locations. DNS can return multiple addresses so clients can connect to an appropriate/available server.

6. You also got this
2404:6800:4009:808::200e

This is an IPv6 address.

Earlier you saw addresses like:

192.178.134.100

Those are IPv4.

So:

192.178.134.100
        ↓
IPv4

and:

2404:6800:4009:808::200e
        ↓
IPv6

For now, focus mainly on IPv4.

7. Let's connect everything together

Your command was:

nslookup google.com

What happened internally?

Think of it like this:

             You
              |
              | nslookup google.com
              ↓
        Your Ubuntu
              |
              ↓
        127.0.0.53
       Local DNS service
              |
              ↓
       Actual DNS server
              |
              ↓
        "google.com"
              |
              ↓
       IP address(es)

And finally you got:

google.com
     ↓
192.178.134.100
192.178.134.101
192.178.134.102
...

That whole process is called DNS Resolution

Resolve = Find the IP address.

=> So when I say:

"nslookup resolved google.com"

I simply mean:

"nslookup found the IP address(es) belonging to google.com."

