Lab 5: Use dig

dig is a very useful DNS troubleshooting command.

Run:

dig google.com

You will see a lot of output.

Focus on this part:

;; ANSWER SECTION:
google.com.    ...    A    142.250.x.x

=> What does this mean?
google.com → A → 142.250.x.x

Meaning:

The IPv4 address of google.com is that IP.

Lab 6: Check your DNS service

Run:

systemctl status systemd-resolved

If it is running, you may see:

Active: active (running)
Meaning
systemd-resolved is running.

=> You can also check:

resolvectl status

This shows DNS information for your network interfaces.

=> In my lab, I run and got the output

bilal-ahmed@bilal-ahmed-VMware-Virtual-Platform:~$ resolvectl status
Global
         Protocols: -LLMNR -mDNS -DNSOverTLS DNSSEC=no/unsupported
  resolv.conf mode: stub

Link 2 (ens33)
    Current Scopes: DNS
         Protocols: +DefaultRoute -LLMNR -mDNS -DNSOverTLS DNSSEC=no/unsupported
Current DNS Server: 192.168.114.2
       DNS Servers: 192.168.114.2
        DNS Domain: localdomain
     Default Route: yes

-----------------------

Explaination:-

1. The most important thing

You have two different addresses here:

127.0.0.53
192.168.114.2

They have different jobs.

127.0.0.53

This is your local DNS listener.

192.168.114.2

This is the actual DNS server your Ubuntu is using

So your DNS flow is approximately:

Ubuntu application
       ↓
127.0.0.53
       ↓
192.168.114.2
       ↓
DNS answer

That's the main thing I want you to understand.

=> So 127.0.0.53 is the local entry point, while 192.168.114.2 is the DNS server that actually handles the query.

2. What does resolv.conf mode: stub mean?

You have:

resolv.conf mode: stub

Here, stub basically means:

/etc/resolv.conf is configured to point applications to a local DNS listener.

And that local listener is:

127.0.0.53

=> So:

/etc/resolv.conf
       ↓
127.0.0.53
       ↓
systemd-resolved
       ↓
192.168.114.2

This is why your /etc/resolv.conf probably contains:

nameserver 127.0.0.53

3. What is ens33?

You have:

Link 2 (ens33)

ens33 is the name of your network interface.

Because you're using VMware, your Ubuntu VM has a virtual network adapter.

You can think of it as:

Ubuntu VM
   |
   └── ens33
         |
         └── Network

You can check it with:

ip addr show ens33

You'll probably see an IP address such as:

192.168.114.xxx

4. What does this mean?
Current Scopes: DNS

Simple meaning:

DNS is available through this network interface.

Your ens33 interface has DNS configuration.

5. Where did 192.168.114.2 probably come from?

Because you're using VMware, 192.168.114.2 is very likely part of your VMware virtual networking setup.

Your VM has a virtual network.

For example:

VMware virtual network

192.168.114.0/24

192.168.114.1
192.168.114.2
192.168.114.xxx

The exact VMware network configuration can vary, but in your case Ubuntu has been given:

DNS server = 192.168.114.2

6. What does Default Route: yes mean?

You have:

Default Route: yes

For DNS purposes, it means:

This network interface is the default interface to use for DNS queries.

