Lab 7: Understand /etc/hosts

Run:

cat /etc/hosts

You may see something like:

127.0.0.1       localhost
127.0.1.1       your-hostname
What is 127.0.0.1?

It means:

This computer itself.

It is called localhost.

So:

127.0.0.1 → localhost

means:

"This name refers to my own computer."

Note:- Important: /etc/hosts is not a DNS server

This is a common beginner confusion.

When you add:

127.0.0.1   myserver

You are not creating a DNS server.

You are only telling your local computer:

"If someone asks for this name, use this IP."

=> Difference
/etc/hosts
    ↓
Local name mapping
DNS server
    ↓
Answers DNS queries for many computers
